WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.940
Okay, so we have this ConvolutionCyclopeptideSequencing algorithm, and

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.970
it works really well on the hypothetical
spectra that we've considered.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.220
But these spectra aren't necessarily

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:17.050
representative of spectra that we might
encounter in practice.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.769
In particular, we had 25% false and
missing masses,

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.610
and that's much less noisy than what we'll
be able to obtain from practical spectra.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.770
Also, I've said that the mass
spectrometer is, we can think

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:36.300
of it as a scale, but it doesn't really
just weigh the peptide fragments;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.640
it's a lot more complicated than that.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.550
What it does is, first it ionizes the
peptide fragments.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.370
So it converts them into ions

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.330
with a positive or negative charge, and
then

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.770
it sorts these fragments using an
electromagnetic field.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.170
Once it does that, it's then able to not
measure

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.260
the mass but the mass/charge ratio of each
fragment.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.900
Then it determines the intensity
(approximately we'll

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.160
think about it as the number of ions)

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.440
at each mass/charge ratio value.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:03.920
And so,

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.350
after it determines this intensity at each
mass/charge

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.630
ratio value, we're able to create a graph
from this.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.870
And so this is really what the spectrum
is.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.570
The spectrum is a graph of intensity on
the Y axis

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.420
and the mass/charge ratio on the X
axis.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.970
So it's detecting give or take,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:25.730
the number of ions at each mass/charge
ratio value.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.130
And from this we somehow need to convert
it into mass, right?

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.230
All we have is mass/charge ratio.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:35.380
We want to consider the most
popular elements in this or

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:39.960
the most intense “peaks” is the term we'll use.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.310
And, the challenge is now to reconstruct,

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:48.890
in this case, to first convert to mass,
and then use those masses to

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:54.320
reconstruct the peptide in a case where we
may not have 25% false and missing masses,

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.330
we may have like 2/3 of the masses that

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.400
are detected in our spectrum may be false,

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.550
in which case we've got to really
switch up what we do,

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.780
and we may need to adapt the algorithms
that we have.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.160
And so, on Stepic, this is where we give
you the example

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.130
to take a real spectrum and try to process
it, and adapt

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.610
the algorithms that you have, maybe come
up with new ideas and

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.490
try to sequence a real peptide from a
real experimental spectrum.

